[section:polynomial_roots Complex Roots of a Polynomial]

``
#include <boost/math/tools/polynomial_roots.hpp>
``

   template <class RandomAccessContainer>
   auto polynomial_roots(RandomAccessContainer const& coefficients);

   template <class RandomAccessContainer>
   auto polynomial_roots_companion_matrix(RandomAccessContainer const& coefficients);

`polynomial_roots` computes all finite complex roots of a polynomial with real,
floating-point coefficients, held in any random access container: `std::vector`, `std::array`,
`std::span` and so on. Both functions return a `std::vector<Complex>`. If the container's
`value_type`, `Real`, is a built-in floating-point type, `Complex` is `std::complex<Real>`; if it is a
Boost.Multiprecision type, `Complex` is the matching multiprecision complex type (`cpp_complex_50` for
`cpp_bin_float_50`, and so on), whose header, e.g. `<boost/multiprecision/cpp_complex.hpp>`, must be
included first. Coefficients are supplied in ascending order, so
`{a0, a1, ..., an}` represents
[@https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polynomial a0 + a1 z + ... + an z^n].
The returned order is unspecified.

The implementation follows the piecewise approximation strategy of Imbach and
Moroz: a Newton-polygon sweep divides the complex plane into radial rings, the
rings are divided into angular sectors, and low-degree Taylor polynomials are
factored locally, with expansions of an order set by the precision of `Real`.
Candidate roots are polished and checked against the input polynomial. A global Aberth iteration is used as a safety net for unresolved or
ill-conditioned clusters.

Exact leading zero coefficients are ignored. Exact trailing zero coefficients
produce the corresponding number of roots at zero. The zero polynomial and
non-finite coefficients cause `std::domain_error` to be thrown.

[heading The companion QR algorithm]

`polynomial_roots_companion_matrix` computes the same roots as the eigenvalues of the companion matrix,
by the core-chasing QR algorithm of Aurentz, Mach, Vandebril and Watkins. It
stores the companion matrix as O(n) 2x2 unitary rotations, so it takes O(n[super 2])
time and O(n) memory, against O(n[super 3]) and O(n[super 2]) for a dense
eigensolver. Before the iteration the variable is scaled so that the constant
and leading coefficients have equal magnitude.

The two functions make different guarantees. `polynomial_roots_companion_matrix` is proven normwise
backward stable: all of its roots are the exact roots of a single polynomial
whose coefficient vector is within a modest multiple of n[epsilon] of the input,
relative to the norm of the coefficients. `polynomial_roots` checks a stronger,
coefficientwise, bound on each root: each one is an exact root of a polynomial
whose coefficients each differ from the input in only the last few bits.
It has no a priori proof of convergence, but it returns only roots that
pass this check. The coefficientwise bound matters when the roots span many
orders of magnitude, as in [prod](z - 10[super -k]): the small roots are
determined by the small coefficients, which a normwise perturbation may change
completely, so `polynomial_roots_companion_matrix` gets the small roots wrong while `polynomial_roots`
gets them to nearly full precision. For roots of comparable magnitude the
two agree.

Exact zero coefficients are handled as for `polynomial_roots`. The zero
polynomial and non-finite coefficients cause `std::domain_error` to be
thrown, and `std::runtime_error` is thrown in the unlikely event that the QR
iteration fails to converge.

[heading Example]

   std::array<double, 3> p{1, 0, 1}; // 1 + z^2
   auto roots = boost::math::tools::polynomial_roots(p);

The result contains approximations to `i` and `-i`.

[heading References]

J. Imbach and G. Moroz, "Fast evaluation and root finding for polynomials with
floating-point coefficients", 2023.

J. L. Aurentz, T. Mach, R. Vandebril and D. S. Watkins, "Fast and backward
stable computation of roots of polynomials", SIAM J. Matrix Anal. Appl. 36
(2015), 942-973.

J. L. Aurentz, T. Mach, L. Robol, R. Vandebril and D. S. Watkins, "Fast and
backward stable computation of roots of polynomials, Part II: backward error
analysis; companion matrix and companion pencil", SIAM J. Matrix Anal. Appl.
39 (2018), 1245-1269.

[endsect]
